{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793570710,"nonce":"ycP4kDdN5PkSwRKkSLQpwpZ9fRU=","title":"LICM and the loop passes skip loops that an unreachable block names as an entry","message":"A block no path reaches can keep a branch into a loop header (an empty forwarding block left when its predecessors were rewired). The loop passes read predecessors off every block's terminator, so the header had a second outside entry and `unique_outside_predecessor` refused it. In scimark SOR the inner loop was skipped in every round: the inlined `(y-1) * width`, `y * width` and `(y+1) * width`, the `y` range tests and the list header loads stayed in the x loop.\n\nTwo changes: LICM ignores predecessors that are not in the dominator tree, and each optimizer round starts with `prune_unreachable_module`. bounds_version and affine_loop keep their own preheader tests; their \"no unique preheader\" skips are not re-checked here.\n\nNUC, 4 pinned cores: SOR Cranelift tier 114 to 110 ms per 3000 cycles; suite A/B (5 interleaved reps of 22 kernels) geomean wall 0.994, SOR -4%, crypto_pyaes -5%, richards -4%, nothing slower than noise.","files":null}]}